<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All the Pencils! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> All the Pencils!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids. reading_2: Show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with the teacher's guidance. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to the correct number of objects when asked and supported with a picture (e.g., Teacher says, 'How many pencils?' and student points to five pencils in a book). Can sequence storybook pictures involving numbers (e.g., placing images of one, two, and three apples in order). Can recognize the letters 'F, G, H, I, and J' in words when supported by corresponding pictures (e.g., 'F' in 'fish').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with "Hello, everyone!" and initiates a quick "Number Song" (e.g., "Ten Little Fingers") to review numbers 1-10 and basic body parts. The teacher holds up real pencils of different colors and counts them aloud, "One red pencil, two yellow pencils, three blue pencils..." asking students to repeat and count along, reinforcing the theme "All the Pencils!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards showing numbers 1-10, clearly enunciating each number. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard with the letter 'F' and says "/f/ /f/ fish" and points to a picture of a fish. The teacher repeats this with letters 'G, H, I, J' and corresponding pictures (e.g., 'G' for 'glue', 'H' for 'hand', 'I' for 'ink', 'J' for 'juice'), modeling the initial sounds and linking them to familiar vocabulary. The teacher also demonstrates how to point to objects while counting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up three fingers) "How many fingers? One, two, three. Is this number four?" (Students shake heads or say "No").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of five pencils) "Is this five pencils?" (Students nod or say "Yes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to letter 'F' flashcard) "Is this 'F' for 'fish'?" (Students point to 'F' or say "Yes").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful storybook titled "Counting Pencils" or "My Ten Favorite Things" (narrative excerpt). The book features pages with clear illustrations showing 1 to 10 of various school supplies (pencils, crayons, markers, glue, blocks) and hands holding up fingers. Each page also prominently displays the number word and numeral. Some pages feature words starting with target letters F, G, H, I, J with corresponding pictures (e.g., 'five', 'glue', 'hand', 'ink', 'juice').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids. Show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with the teacher's guidance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher shows the cover and asks, "What do you think this story is about? Is it about pencils or cars?" Students point to the pencils on the cover. "Is it about counting?" (Students nod).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Reading:</b> As the teacher reads each page, they pause and ask, "How many pencils here?" or "What number is this?" Students point to the objects and the numeral on the page. "Can you find the letter 'G' in 'glue'?" (Teacher points to the word 'glue' and students point to 'G').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False with visuals:</b> Teacher points to a page showing seven crayons and says, "This page has six crayons. True or False?" (Students indicate 'False' by shaking their heads or giving a thumbs down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading numbers (1-10) and basic familiar words (pencil, crayon, block, hand, finger, glue, ink, juice). Grammar: Quantity + nouns (e.g., 'ten fingers', 'three pencils'). Wh-questions ('How many?'). Yes/No questions. Pronunciation: Initial /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers'. Phonemic awareness: Recognizing number sounds (1-10) and recognizing letter sounds (F, G, H, I, J).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Reading & Matching:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with two columns: one column has numbers (1-10) and the other has pictures of varying quantities of pencils, crayons, or blocks. Students draw a line from the number to the correct picture, practicing number recognition and quantity matching. Teacher circulates, saying numbers and objects aloud: "Match number five to five pencils." "Is this three?" (pointing to a picture of three blocks).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Reading & Pointing:</b> The teacher displays large flashcards or projected images from the storybook, each showing a specific number of items (e.g., "Two red crayons," "Eight blue blocks"). Students are given individual mini whiteboards or laminated cards with numbers. The teacher reads a phrase (e.g., "Show me six yellow pencils") and students read the number in their mind, then hold up the corresponding number card. The teacher then says, "Point to the letter 'I' in 'ink'!" (Students point to the letter on a flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Picture Sequencing:</b> In pairs or small groups, students receive a set of three-picture story strips (e.g., a card with one pencil, a card with two pencils, a card with three pencils) from the "All the Pencils!" story. The teacher guides them to put the cards in numerical order by asking, "What comes first? One pencil. What comes next? Two pencils." Students collaborate to physically arrange the pictures in sequence, verbally identifying the number of items on each card.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "My Counting Book" Reading and Sequencing Activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual or paired reading and pointing activity using a simplified "My Counting Book" worksheet or small booklets prepared by the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students will actively read, point to quantities of pencils and school supplies, and demonstrate sequencing skills, directly linking to "All the Pencils!" and implicitly "Hold Up Ten Fingers!" by showing the counted quantity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in identifying numbers and objects, collaborative interaction (if paired), and communicative task completion through pointing and simple verbal responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes individual "My Counting Book" worksheets or simple booklets. Each page has a number (1-10) and a corresponding quantity of items (pencils, crayons, blocks). Some pages include a target letter (F, G, H, I, J) with its corresponding word and picture (e.g., 'F' for 'five').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to "read" each page by pointing to the number, then pointing to and counting the objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, asking questions: "How many pencils do you see here?" (Students point and count). "What number is this?" (Students point to the number). "Can you find the letter 'J' on this page?" (Students point to 'J' in 'juice').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For sequencing, students are given a strip of three mixed-up picture cards (e.g., 2 pencils, 1 pencil, 3 pencils) and asked to arrange them in the correct numerical order (1, 2, 3) on their desk, verbally explaining "one, two, three" as they place them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to show their work to a peer, demonstrating their ability to count and identify numbers and objects.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Counting Book" activity and a simple "Point and Identify" exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students independently complete a "Count & Circle" worksheet. The worksheet displays rows of varying quantities of objects (e.g., four crayons, seven blocks) and a choice of three numbers below each row. Students circle the correct number matching the quantity. Additionally, a section asks students to circle specific letters (F, G, H, I, J) shown alongside simple pictures (e.g., 'F' next to a fish).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes numbers 1-10 with visual support (pointing/circling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Points to and identifies correct quantity of objects (e.g., pencils, crayons).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows understanding of simple sequencing (e.g., placing three pictures in order 1, 2, 3).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes target letters (F, G, H, I, J) when presented with a word/picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates in counting aloud and answering quantity questions with simple responses.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a green happy face and a red sad face. "Was counting the pencils easy or a little hard today?" (Students point to the face that best reflects their feeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me five fingers?" (Students hold up five fingers as they leave the class, a quick final check).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite number to count in our storybook today?" (Students point to a number on a visual aid or say the number).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which letter did you like to find? F, G, H, I, or J?" (Students point to the letter or say it).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in today's reading lesson? The storybook visuals were highly engaging and helped students connect numbers to quantities. The hands-on sequencing activity fostered collaborative learning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with distinguishing 'six' and 'seven' when counting aloud; more repetition with TPR will be beneficial. I need to ensure more varied opportunities for students to verbalize the target letters.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and recognizing numbers, objects, and letters. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this recognition by having students actively speak and interact, asking and answering "How many?" questions about the same objects and demonstrating letter sounds verbally in a more interactive setting.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>